configured, but in real deployment they count for approximately 15%-20%; Other parameters such as timers or constants will count for less than 5%; It is highly possible that the SIB1 size would exceed the 2976 bits limitation if network wants to configure multiple SI/ PLMN ID list/ UAC list and support extra features like RedCap. SIB1 split and impact on performance Based on the analysis on size of NR SIB1, it can be seen that 6G SIB1 split may be needed. During RAN2#132[2] and RAN2#133[3], RAN2 discussed that the study of SIB1 split should take system performance into account. According to the current RRC specification[4], the default transmission repetition periodicity of SIB1 is 20 ms. Thus, the worst-case of access latency for a UE to acquire SIB1 is up to its periodicity (i.e., 20 ms) under ideal channel conditions. For the SIBs other than SIB1(OSI), the ones that have the same periodicity can be mapped to the same SI message. Each SI message is transmitted within a periodically occurring SI-window, and the SI-windows of different SI messages do not overlap. Consequently, if SIB1 is split and part of them are moved to a new SIB (e.g., subSIB1) and transmitted within a SI-window similar as OSI, in worst-case the latency for a UE to acquire the complete SIB1 will be the periodicity of SI message containing subSIB1. The following figure shows an example. Figure 3. An example for latency for the UE to acquire SIB1 and subSIB1 Since subSIB1 is regarded as part of the RMSI, configuring a large subSIB1 periodicity is not expected. Therefore, with a typical short SI periodicity of 80 ms as follows[4], in worst-case the latency would increase by 80 ms due to the SIB1 split. Observation 3: When SIB1 is split and part of it is moved to OSI, in worst case the latency to 

{"chunk_id":"R2-1914381-005","collection":"ran2_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Qualcomm"],"tdocNumber":"R2-1914381","title":"Discussion on fast SCell activation based on Aperiodic TRS (A-TRS)"},"point_id":"8252820226435612467"}
In this contribution, we discuss how to speed up SCell activation based on A-TRS. We propose: Observation 1: According to RAN4 reply LS, the main latency contributor of NR SCell activation latency is Tactivation_time which includes AGC setting and fine synchronization, cell detection (only for unknown SCell) and L1-RSRP measurement with beam management and reporting (only for FR2). Observation 2: Almost all major latency contributors of Tactivation_time rely on SSB at least in NR Rel-15. Under typical SMTC configuration (20ms), NR SCell activation latency is even longer than LTE, which is because SSB (typical 20ms) is much sparser than CRS (every 1ms TTI) in LTE. Observation 3: Both RAN1 and RAN4 response were positive on supporting temporary RS from both latency benefit and spec change feasibility. Observation 4: ‘Dormancy’ behaviour and temporary RS are target for different scenarios: The temporary RS method reduces the latency of transition from deactivated state to activated state The ‘dormancy’ behaviour reduces the latency of the transition from ‘dormancy’ to SCell utilisation for data transfer under activated state Observation 5: If TRS configured by gNB, UE can utilize TRS burst for AGC adjustment and FTL/TTL tracking when SSB is not present. Observation 6: Aperiodic TRS (A-TRS) was agreed to be supported for FR2 in RAN1#92b and FR1 in RAN1#93. Thus, TRS can be triggered by DCI to further reduce latency of the UE performing AGC. Observation 7: A-TRS can achieve similar latency reduction performance as dormant BWP, but can provide further benefits in below scenarios: The UE can stay in the deactivated state when there is no data to transmit / receive Less frequent activation of SCell SSB is not present for AGC and FTL/TTL Proposal 1: Considering ‘dormancy’ behaviour and temporary RS are target for different scenarios of reducing latency, also support temporary RS as solution to speed up SCell activation from deactivated state to activated state. Proposal 2: Introduce A-TRS as the solution to reduce NR SCell activation latency of transition from deactivated state to activated state. Proposal 3: The specific signalling design of A-TRS is left to RAN1.
